## Third-party connector brand icons

The console displays small brand glyphs to identify third-party services a
connector can connect to (e.g. Amazon, GitHub, Spotify). All product names,
trademarks, and registered trademarks referenced or depicted by these icons
are the property of their respective owners. These icons are used by the
PDP-Connect project strictly to identify the products and companies a
connector integrates with; their use implies no sponsorship, affiliation, or
endorsement by the mark holder.

Two sources of icon are in play:

- Icons bundled with `@pdpp/polyfill-connectors`' shipped connector
  manifests (`manifests/icons/*.svg`, referenced by each manifest's
  `brand.icon` field). That package is an external dependency of this repo,
  not vendored source here.
- When a connector's manifest declares no bundled icon, the console falls
  back to a same-name lookup in `simple-icons` (https://simpleicons.org), an
  offline npm package of ~3,300 brand SVGs. simple-icons' SVG markup is
  released under CC0 1.0, but per its own LICENSE.md that waiver explicitly
  does not extend to trademark or patent rights, and the project maintains a
  brand-owner takedown channel. See
  https://github.com/simple-icons/simple-icons/blob/master/DISCLAIMER.md.

A connector with no icon from either source renders a deterministic
monogram (the connector's initial(s) and a color derived from its name) —
this is the normal, first-class default, not an error state.
